# [6LO](../wg/6lo.html)

## Summary

The 6LO Working Group met at IETF 127. The session included working group status updates, discussions on advanced drafts nearing publication (PASA and GAO), updates on active working group documents (`draft-ietf-6lo-schc-15dot4`, `draft-ietf-6lo-owc`, and `draft-ietf-6lo-rfc8163-bis`), and proposals for two new topics: handling 6LoWPAN payload length with L2 padding and IPv6 transmission over Controller Area Network (CAN).

Additionally, the chairs announced Area Director leadership changes: Éric Vyncke is stepping down as AD, with Jen Linkova taking over the role for the working group.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Working Group Administration and Document Status
- **Presentation:** [6lo chairs introduction](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-6lo-chairs-introduction-01)
- **Presenters:** Laurent Toutain, Carles Gomez

Laurent Toutain and Carles Gomez presented the status of the working group documents. Shweta Bhandare is stepping down as co-chair. Éric Vyncke is transitioning out as Area Director, with Jen Linkova joining as the new AD. Éric Vyncke noted that he will continue as the responsible AD to shepherd the PASA and GAO drafts through the publication process.

### Path-Aware Semantic Addressing (PASA) and Generic Address Assignment Option (GAO)
- **Presentations:**
  - [Path-Aware Semantic Addressing (PASA) for Low power and Lossy Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-path-aware-semantic-addressing-pasa-for-low-power-and-lossy-networks-00)
  - [Generic Address Assignment Option for 6LoWPAN Neighbor Discovery](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-generic-address-assignment-option-for-6lowpan-neighbor-discovery-01)
- **Presenter:** Luigi Iannone

Luigi Iannone presented updates addressing AD review feedback:
- **PASA:** Updated to Experimental status. Experiment goals, success criteria, and forwarding performance target comparisons (e.g., against RPL without full routing state overhead) were added. The comprehensive use cases section was moved to an appendix.
- **GAO:** Revised the scope and introduction to clarify that GAO is intended for specific 6LoWPAN/RFC 8505 deployments with distributed address allocation algorithms (such as PASA) and inefficient L2 multicast. The appendix was restructured to present a neutral comparison between GAO, DHCPv6, and SLAAC.

### Transmission of SCHC-compressed Packets over IEEE 802.15.4 Networks
- **Presentation:** [Transmission of SCHC-compressed packets over IEEE 802.15.4 networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-transmission-of-schc-compressed-packets-over-ieee-802154-networks-01)
- **Presenter:** Carles Gomez
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-6lo-schc-15dot4`

Carles Gomez reviewed updates in version -15:
- Aligned terminology with the SCHC architecture draft (versions 06 and 07). Alexander Pelov confirmed that terminology definitions have stabilized.
- Improved the Pointer-Based Route-Over (PRO) mode header by explicitly introducing a 2-bit ECN field (from Traffic Class) so intermediate routers without SCHC decompression rules can read and modify ECN bits.
- Moved the normative address residue length table from Appendix C into Section 4.3.
- Éric Vyncke and Alexander Pelov recommended sending the Working Group Last Call (WGLC) announcement to the SCHC WG mailing list as well.

### Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Short-Range Optical Wireless Communications
- **Presentation:** [Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Short-Range Optical Wireless Communications](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-transmission-of-ipv6-packets-over-short-range-optical-wireless-communications-00)
- **Presenter:** Young-Hwan Choi
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-6lo-owc`

Young-Hwan Choi summarized updates in version -08, including formatting changes for 6CIO (6LoWPAN Capability Indication Option) flags, normative language formatting, and reference updates. Laurent Toutain and Carles Gomez noted that carrying SCHC capability signaling in 6CIO might be worth evaluating as a generic mechanism in a separate draft rather than tying it exclusively to OWC.

### Transmission of IPv6 over MS/TP Networks
- **Presentation:** [Transmission of IPv6 over Multidrop Serial Bus/Token Passing (MS/TP) Networks [draft-ietf-6lo-rfc8163-bis-01]](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-transmission-of-ipv6-over-multidrop-serial-bustoken-passing-mstp-networks-draft-ietf-6lo-rfc8163-bis-01-00)
- **Presenter:** Carles Gomez (presented on behalf of Carrie Anne Higgins)
- **Draft:** `draft-ietf-6lo-rfc8163-bis`

Key updates presented for `draft-ietf-6lo-rfc8163-bis`:
- Terminology aligned with ANSI/ASHRAE 135-2024.
- Adjusted maximum MSDU payload length from 1509 to 1510 octets to account for a degenerate case where an uncompressed IPv6 header with a dispatch byte adds an extra byte.
- Added reproducible test cases using Scapy in Appendix D.
- Implementation testbed constructed using Nordic nRF52 and RIOT OS (tested overnight with 60,000 pings without loss).
- Wireshark dissector merged to decode MS/TP frame type 34 (6LoWPAN).
- Dave Robin supported the work and noted that BACnet/ASHRAE will process errata regarding frame type 34 reservation.
- It was agreed to decouple SCHC over MS/TP into a separate document rather than delaying the RFC 8163-bis update.

### 6LoWPAN Payload Length Handling with L2 Padding
- **Presentation:** [6LoWPAN Payload Length Handling with L2 Padding](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-6lowpan-payload-length-handling-with-l2-padding-00)
- **Presenter:** Adnan Rashid

Adnan Rashid introduced a problem occurring when 6LoWPAN frames traverse L2 technologies that require padding (e.g., Ethernet switches/bridges). Because 6LoWPAN compression elides the IPv6 payload length field, receiving nodes reconstructing packets over padded L2 frames cannot correctly determine the original IPv6 payload length. The proposed solution introduces an Explicit Payload Length Header (EPLH) field based on RFC 8025/8066 format. Luigi Iannone and Carles Gomez agreed to review the draft on the mailing list.

### Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Controller Area Network (CAN)
- **Presentation:** [Transmission of IPv6 Packets over Controller Area Network](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6lo-transmission-of-ipv6-packets-over-controller-area-network-01)
- **Presenter:** Jung-Soo Park

Jung-Soo Park presented a revived draft defining an IPv6 adaptation layer for Controller Area Network (CAN) using 29-bit extended CAN IDs, ISO-TP for fragmentation/reassembly, and 14-bit addressing. Key updates since previous versions include moving header compression to SCHC, adding support for CAN FD, removing remote frames, and addressing privacy issues. 
- In the chat log, Christian Amsüss pointed out an existing RIOT OS CAN implementation (DOSE driver), while Hannes Tschofenig noted automotive transitions to Single Pair Ethernet (T1).
- Open questions regarding CAN FD padding, single-frame escapes, SCHC rule sets, and 29-bit CAN ID coexistence will be taken to the mailing list.

